# =============================================================================
#  assets/textures/parts_creature.pix  —  a cast of eighteen, out of twelve tiles
# =============================================================================
#  Not one of these twelve tiles is a creature. Three bodies, three faces, three
#  crests, three tails — and every species in assets/creatures/species.def is two to
#  four of them stacked plus two colour swaps.
#
#  That is the trade chapter 135 opened the fourth `.hrt` door to make, taken at the
#  scale it was opened for. Eighteen drawings, kept consistent by hand, is a job
#  nobody finishes; eighteen recipes over one sheet is consistent by construction,
#  and the nineteenth costs four lines. What it costs in return is freedom: these
#  creatures will always be built out of the same twelve shapes, and that shows.
#
#  EVOLUTION IS THE SAME RECIPE PLUS A PART. Stage one is a body and a face. Stage
#  two adds the line's crest. Stage three adds its tail. Nothing is redrawn and the
#  colour never moves, so a creature growing up reads as the same animal — which is
#  the one thing a mixer gives you that a brush charges for.
#
#  THE PARTS DO NOT OVERLAP, AND THAT IS A DESIGN CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN A HABIT.
#  The first version of this sheet put the bodies at row 3 and the crests at rows
#  1..4, so the body — which is composited LAST — buried them: the water line's
#  stage two and stage three came out pixel-identical to stage one, and the single
#  claim this sheet exists to make was invisible in the render. The bands below are
#  what fixed it, and they are the file's actual contract:
#
#      crests  rows 0..4          bodies  rows 5..15, cols 1..12
#      tails   cols 11..15        faces   eyes row 7-8 (cols 3-4, 9-10),
#                                         mouth row 10 (cols 4..9)
#
#  A body covers where a crest and a tail JOIN it, and nothing more. Add a part
#  outside its band and the composite is still valid — it is just a picture where
#  something is missing, which no parser can tell you about.
#
#  Two colours carry the whole palette: `c` is the body and `a` is the accent, and
#  every `.mix` swaps exactly those two. The outline, the eyes and the mouth are
#  NEVER swapped, which is why they are separate palette entries rather than
#  convenient shades of the body: a swap matches exact RGB, so a colour shared
#  between two roles would move both.
#
#  Parts stack in this order in every mix: tail, crest, body, face.
# =============================================================================
size 16
grid 4 3

palette . 00000000
palette o 23262e      # outline  — never swapped
palette c 6f7686      # body     <- swapped by every .mix
palette a b9c0cf      # accent   <- swapped by every .mix
palette e f2f4f8      # eye
palette p 1a1c22      # pupil    — never swapped
palette m 3a3f4b      # mouth    — never swapped


